  "title": "Pulled Over: A Traffic Police Stop",
  "category": "work-admin",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 7,
  "order": 704,
  "theme": "police",
  "culturalNote": "Traffic police stops are routine on Kenyan roads: the officer (address them as \"afande\") checks your licence, insurance sticker and the car's lights and tyres. Stay calm, polite and patient. You may hear a hint about \"kitu kidogo\" (something small) — a bribe; the safe, legal answer is to politely accept the official process (a fine or court date) and never offer money. Verb extensions run through every line: simamisha (make stop), nimesimamishwa (I've been stopped), umefanyiwa ukaguzi (you've had an inspection done on you).",
  "startingPoint": "A traffic officer waves you to the side of the road near Westlands. You're driving.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "verb-extensions",
    "discourse-particles",
    "past-li"
  ],
